# Line endings.
#
# This matters more than it looks. Git for Windows ships with core.autocrlf=true,
# so without this file every .sh in the repo is checked out with CRLF - and the
# seed builder embeds those files with ReadAllBytes, byte for byte, straight into
# the guest. The VM then boots and dies on the first line:
#
#     /usr/local/lib/vm-bootstrap/_lib.sh: line 2: $'\r': command not found
#
# base64 in the seed protects a script in transit, but it faithfully preserves a
# CRLF that was already there. The repo works on the machine that wrote the files
# with LF and breaks on every machine that clones it, which is the worst way for
# this to fail.
#
# So: anything the GUEST reads or executes is pinned to LF. PowerShell and
# Markdown are left to Git's normal handling, since they only ever run on the
# host.

# Sensible default: normalise text in the repo, decide checkout per-pattern.
* text=auto

# ---- everything that ends up inside the VM ---------------------------------
*.sh            text eol=lf
*.tmpl          text eol=lf
*.service       text eol=lf
*.rules         text eol=lf
*.pkla          text eol=lf
*.cfg           text eol=lf
*.env           text eol=lf

# The bootstrap runner and the systemd unit have no extension between them, so
# name them outright.
blueprints/bootstrap/_runner.sh             text eol=lf
blueprints/bootstrap/_lib.sh                text eol=lf
blueprints/bootstrap/_vm-bootstrap.service  text eol=lf

# Config is read by the PowerShell YAML reader on the host, but it is also the
# file people paste between machines. LF keeps it identical everywhere.
*.yaml          text eol=lf
*.yml           text eol=lf

# ---- host-side only --------------------------------------------------------
*.ps1           text
*.psm1          text
*.psd1          text
*.md            text

# ---- never touch -----------------------------------------------------------
*.iso           binary
*.vhdx          binary
*.png           binary
